`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"One Llama 3 configuration frames the lecture",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The Llama 3 report gives one configuration of its dense 405B model that combines four of the six splits ",e.jsx(s,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"}),". The four are the batch (DP 128), the matrices (TP 8), the layers (PP 16) and the training state (FSDP). We use the 16,384-GPU, 8K-context, BF16 row from version 3 of the report, dated 2024-11-23. It sets tensor parallelism to 8, pipeline parallelism to 16 and data parallelism to 128. Context parallelism is 1, so the sequence is not split at this length. The degrees multiply to the number of GPUs: ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"TP × CP × PP × DP = 8 × 1 × 16 × 128 = 16,384"}),". The training state is sharded with FSDP, the arithmetic runs in BF16, and the run reaches a model FLOPs utilization (MFU) of 41%. The model is dense, so it has no experts to split."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Most tabs decode one field of the card, and the Synthesis tab reads the whole card. The same reasoning scales down to what you run this week: a 500M model on 4 GPUs."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The row comes from one run. Table 4 of the report also lists other rows with different degrees, and combining fields from different rows describes a configuration the report never ran. One run also cannot isolate which of its choices mattered. Treat the card as a dated worked example."})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Where this fits: distributed.py",reading:!0,accent:6,number:"7",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
