`]}),e.jsx(f,{compact:!0,initialOp:"broadcast"}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Collectives move data between all ranks of a group",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Every split makes the GPUs exchange data, so the price of a split starts with the price of moving bytes. Distributed training moves its data with collective operations, or collectives: communication steps that every rank of a process group takes part in together. Every rank of the group makes the same call, and each collective is defined by what the ranks hold before it runs and after. The NVIDIA Collective Communications Library (NCCL) implements the collectives on NVIDIA GPUs and runs them over NVLink inside a node and InfiniBand between nodes. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"torch.distributed"})," exposes them in PyTorch."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A collective moves and combines data, but it does not average. An all-reduce with SUM leaves the elementwise sum on every rank, and a reduce-scatter leaves each rank one shard of it. When training needs the average gradient, it must divide the summed result by N, or scale each rank's contribution by 1/N before the reduction."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The explorer animates each collective on four GPUs. Its first frame shows what each GPU holds before the collective and its last frame what it holds after, so comparing the two gives the operation's definition."})]})]}),e.jsx(h,{caption:"The reduce-scatter leaves each GPU one reduced shard. The all-gather that follows gives every GPU the full reduced tensor, the same end state as the all-reduce run directly. The readout gives 1.5 GB sent per GPU for a 1 GB buffer on four GPUs.",children:e.jsx(f,{compact:!0,initialOp:"all-reduce",initialShowIdentity:!0})}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Eight collectives, before and after",reading:!0,accent:1,number:"10",children:[e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Collective"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Before"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"After"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"broadcast"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"one rank has the tensor"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"every rank has a copy"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"scatter"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"one rank has all pieces"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"each rank has one piece"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"gather"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"each rank has one piece"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"one rank has all pieces"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"reduce"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"each rank has a tensor"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"one rank has the sum"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"all-reduce"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"each rank has a tensor"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"every rank has the sum"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"reduce-scatter"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"each rank has a tensor"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"each rank has one shard of the sum"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"all-gather"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"each rank has one shard"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"every rank has every shard"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"all-to-all"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"each rank has a piece for every rank"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"each rank has the pieces sent to it"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Each collective is defined by who holds what",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A broadcast copies one rank's tensor to every rank. A scatter cuts one rank's tensor into pieces and sends one piece to each rank, and a gather collects the pieces back onto one rank. A reduce combines the tensors of all ranks elementwise, with a sum, a maximum or another operation, and leaves the result on one rank. An all-reduce leaves the result on every rank: its end state is that of a reduce followed by a broadcast. A reduce-scatter also reduces, but each rank keeps only its own distinct shard of the result. An all-gather starts from one shard per rank and ends with the full tensor on every rank, reducing nothing. In an all-to-all, every rank sends a distinct piece to every other rank. Week 13 uses it to send tokens to the GPUs that hold their experts. These before and after states are what your DDP and ZeRO-1 implementations must preserve."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Six of the operations form three pairs by where the result ends up: broadcast and all-reduce replicate it, gather and reduce concentrate it on one rank, and scatter and reduce-scatter shard it. In each pair, one operation reduces and the other only moves values that already exist."})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"A ring all-reduce sends under 2S per GPU, for any N",accent:2,number:"11",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["A ring ",e.jsx(y,{name:"all-reduce",children:"all-reduce"}),": reduce-scatter, then all-gather"]}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["2(N−1) steps ",e.jsx(s,{id:"patarasuk2009ring",children:"Patarasuk & Yuan, 2009"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Per GPU: approaches 2S as N grows"}),`
`]}),e.jsx(g,{math:"\\text{bytes per GPU} = 2S\\,\\frac{N-1}{N}",label:"(1)"}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"The ring sends 2S(N − 1)/N bytes per GPU",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The standard algorithm for an all-reduce is the ring. Arrange the N GPUs in a ring, each sending to one neighbor and receiving from the other. Let S be the size in bytes of the full tensor each GPU holds. The ring cuts it into N chunks of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"S/N"})," bytes and runs in two phases. The first, a reduce-scatter, takes N − 1 steps. In each step every GPU sends one chunk to its neighbor, which adds it to its own copy of that chunk. Afterwards each GPU holds one fully reduced chunk and has sent ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"S(N − 1)/N"})," bytes. The second, an all-gather, passes the reduced chunks around the ring in another N − 1 steps with the same volume, until every GPU holds every reduced chunk. Together the phases take ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"2(N − 1)"})," steps, and each GPU sends ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"2S(N − 1)/N"})," bytes ",e.jsx(s,{id:"patarasuk2009ring",children:"Patarasuk & Yuan, 2009"}),". Formula (1) is this derived count, under the ring model."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The factor ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"(N − 1)/N"})," is below 1 for every N and approaches 1 as N grows, so each GPU always sends less than 2S, and adding GPUs hardly changes what it sends. Patarasuk and Yuan show that in any all-reduce some GPU must send at least this much, so the ring, in which every GPU sends exactly this much, is bandwidth-optimal ",e.jsx(s,{id:"patarasuk2009ring",children:"Patarasuk & Yuan, 2009"}),"."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Take N = 4 and S = 1 GB. The ring has six steps. Each GPU sends 0.75 GB during the reduce-scatter and 0.75 GB during the all-gather, 1.5 GB in all, the figure the explorer's all-reduce view reports. Summed over the four GPUs, the cluster sends 6 GB."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The formula counts bytes under the ring model, and NCCL does not always run a ring. Every step also pays a fixed startup cost, its latency, whatever the size of the message. For small tensors this latency can dominate, and a tree algorithm, which needs fewer rounds, can be faster. For large buffers the sustained bandwidth matters more. NCCL chooses among its algorithms by message size and topology. So formula (1) gives the volume each GPU sends, and a measured time depends on more than that volume."}),e.jsx(t.h4,{children:"An all-reduce is a reduce-scatter followed by an all-gather"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ring also shows the identity that the rest of the lecture rests on: an all-reduce is a reduce-scatter followed by an all-gather. The reduce-scatter applies the reduction elementwise and leaves each rank one distinct reduced shard of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"S/N"})," bytes. The all-gather then exchanges these shards without reducing them again, and every rank ends with the same full reduced tensor that an all-reduce would have produced. With SUM, the result is still a sum, so dividing by N remains a separate step of training. The two halves can run together or apart. DDP runs them together on the gradients, as one all-reduce. ZeRO separates them, so that the training state stays sharded, and the ZeRO & FSDP tab returns to the identity to price ZeRO's communication."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:'The explorer on the slide "Collectives say who holds what, before and after" checks the identity on four GPUs with equal chunks. In its all-reduce view, the toggle "RS + AG = all-reduce identity" shows the reduced shards after the reduce-scatter, then the full tensor after the all-gather, which matches the all-reduce run directly. The view says nothing about speed: it leaves out tensor shapes, topology, contention, launch latency and overlap.'})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"NVLink is about nine times faster than the network",accent:3,number:"12",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
