`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"DDP needs a process group, one all-reduce and a division",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["DDP needs only the calls on the slide. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"dist.init_process_group"})," joins the processes into one group and starts the backend that carries their collectives: NCCL on GPUs, or Gloo when the tests run on the CPU. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"dist.get_rank()"})," returns the process's rank, and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"dist.get_world_size()"})," returns N, which the code calls ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"world"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["After the backward pass, each rank holds the gradient of the loss on its own slice of the batch. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"dist.all_reduce(grad, op=dist.ReduceOp.SUM)"})," replaces every rank's gradient with the sum over all ranks, so ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"grad.div_(world)"})," divides it by N to give the averaged gradient that DDP uses. SUM is also what ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"dist.all_reduce"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"dist.reduce_scatter"})," apply when you pass no ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"op"}),", so leaving out the argument does not change the need to divide."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The division has a reason. Suppose the loss is the mean over the tokens of the batch, and every rank's slice holds the same number of them. The mean over the whole batch is then the mean of the N slice means, so its gradient is the average of the N slice gradients. After the division, every rank holds the gradient that one GPU would compute on the full batch. Without it, every rank would hold N times that gradient."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"That equality is also the test. Run the same model, initial weights and batch once on one GPU and once on N ranks, and compare the gradients or parameters after a step. They should agree up to floating-point rounding, since the two runs add the same contributions in a different order. In the exercise you verify your DDP against such a single-GPU reference."})]})]})]}),`
