`,e.jsx(t.span,{"data-line":"",children:e.jsx(t.span,{style:{"--shiki-dark":"#E6EDF3","--shiki-light":"#1F2328"},children:"dist.all_gather(param_chunks, param_shard)"})})]})})}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"The strategies differ in where the two halves run",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"DDP and the ZeRO stages are built from the same two collectives, a reduce-scatter and an all-gather. They differ in which tensors the halves act on and in what happens between them. DDP runs both halves back to back on the gradients, which together form one all-reduce. ZeRO-1 and ZeRO-2 reduce-scatter the gradients, update each rank's shard of the parameters, and then all-gather the updated parameters. Fully sharded FSDP2 gathers each layer's weights when needed and reshards them after use."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['Llama 3 falls between these. Section 3.3.2 of its report says that its use of FSDP "shards optimizer states and gradients, but for model shards we do not reshard after forward computation to avoid an extra all-gather communication during backward passes" ',e.jsx(s,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"}),". In PyTorch's FSDP2 this choice is the setting ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"reshard_after_forward=False"}),": the gathered parameters stay resident from forward until backward, which costs parameter memory and saves the second gather. We call this ZeRO-2-style, an analogy about what stays resident: the system is FSDP, but like ZeRO-2 it keeps full parameters through forward and backward."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The code on this slide writes ZeRO-1's step with raw collectives. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"dist.reduce_scatter"})," takes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"grad_chunks"}),", the local gradient cut into N chunks, and writes into ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"grad_shard"})," the sum over all ranks of this rank's chunk. Like a raw ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"all_reduce"}),", it returns sums. So ZeRO-1 must divide its shard by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"world"})," before the update, which gives it the same averaged gradient slice that DDP computes. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"update_param_shard"})," stands for your optimizer step on the shard, from ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"grad_shard"})," and the rank's ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"optimizer_state_shard"}),". ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"dist.all_gather"})," then collects every rank's updated ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"param_shard"})," into ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"param_chunks"}),", so that every rank holds the same full parameters again."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"In the exercise you build ZeRO-1 on these calls, next to DDP. You wrap your Week 5 AdamW so that each rank holds exactly 1/N of the optimizer state, and you verify it as you verify DDP: against a single-GPU reference run with the same model, initial weights and batch, whose parameters after a step it must match up to floating-point rounding."})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Fully sharded FSDP2 gathers per layer",accent:4,width:"wide",number:"22",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
