`]}),e.jsx(c,{strategy:"tp"}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Column then row keeps GELU local and needs one all-reduce each way",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["ZeRO shards the storage of the training state, but every GPU still runs every layer on the full activations of its own slice of the batch. That is not enough when the activations of a single layer, or a single matrix multiplication, are too large for one GPU. Tensor parallelism (TP) shards the computation instead. It splits the weight matrices of each layer across GPUs, and each GPU computes its part of the layer. The idea goes back to the intra-layer model parallelism of Megatron-LM ",e.jsx(s,{id:"shoeybi2019megatron",children:"Shoeybi et al., 2019"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The MLP of Week 2 shows how the split works. Let ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"B"})," be the batch size, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"S"})," the sequence length (no longer the byte count of formula (1)), ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"D"})," the model width, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_ff"})," the MLP width and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"t"})," the TP degree, the number of GPUs that share each layer. The input is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"X:[B,S,D]"}),", and the MLP computes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"GELU(X W_1) W_2"})," with ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"W_1:[D,d_ff]"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"W_2:[d_ff,D]"}),". Both matrices are split along the same ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_ff"})," coordinates, which needs ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_ff"})," to be divisible by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"t"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The up-projection is split by columns. Rank ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"r"})," holds ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"W_1^(r):[D,d_ff/t]"})," and computes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"X W_1^(r):[B,S,d_ff/t]"}),", a complete slice of the hidden activation. GELU acts on each element separately, so it applies to that slice with no communication. The down-projection is split by rows to match. Rank ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"r"})," holds ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"W_2^(r):[d_ff/t,D]"}),", the rows that multiply its hidden slice, and computes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"GELU(X W_1^(r)) W_2^(r):[B,S,D]"}),". This is a partial sum: the MLP output is the sum of the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"t"})," partial outputs. One all-reduce over the TP group adds them and leaves the full output on every rank. The backward pass mirrors this. Every rank used the whole input ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"X"}),", so each yields a partial gradient for ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"X"})," of shape ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[B,S,D]"}),", and one all-reduce sums them. The MLP therefore needs one all-reduce forward and one backward."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The order of the two splits matters. Split the up-projection by rows instead, and each rank computes a partial sum of the pre-activation. GELU cannot act on partial sums, because in general ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"GELU(a + b) ≠ GELU(a) + GELU(b)"}),". So the ranks must sum them before the nonlinearity, on the critical path. After the down-projection, now split by columns, each rank holds only some columns of the output, which must be joined a second time. The wrong order needs two synchronizations in the forward pass where the right order needs one."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In the diagram, the two lanes are the two ranks of a TP group. Unlike the DDP lanes, which received their own slices of the batch, both get the same input ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"X"}),". The ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"ALL-REDUCE"})," stage sums their partial outputs into the full output ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Z"}),'. The "Attention" view shows the attention block in the same lanes. Each rank computes its own heads with no communication, because the heads are independent, and its row slice of the output projection leaves a partial output that one all-reduce sums, as in the MLP. Both views are two-rank sketches of the forward pass.']})]})]}),e.jsx(h,{caption:"With the row split first, the lanes hold partial sums that must be summed before GELU, and the column slices of the output must be joined at the end. That makes two synchronizations in the forward pass instead of one.",children:e.jsx(c,{strategy:"tp",initialWrongOrder:!0})}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"KV heads must divide evenly across TP ranks",reading:!0,accent:3,number:"29",items:"3",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Each GPU computes whole heads"}),`
