`]}),e.jsx(d,{controls:["model","gpus","shard","tp"]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"TP shards selected regions inside one eight-GPU node",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The Llama 3 card sets TP = 8. In its cluster, the eight ranks of each TP group occupy one eight-GPU NVLink domain, a single node ",e.jsx(s,{id:"llama3-2024",children:"Grattafiori et al., 2024"}),", so TP's per-layer all-reduces stay on the fastest links. The degree follows this cluster's topology, and a cluster with another node size or link hierarchy places TP differently. TP can also span nodes, usually at a higher cost."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Within its group, TP shards the weight matrices of the attention and MLP blocks, their gradients and optimizer state, and the activations inside those blocks. The residual stream and the normalization and dropout regions stay replicated on every rank. Memory per GPU therefore falls by less than the factor ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"t"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The ledger's saving is idealized: every band shrinks by the full factor ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"t"}),", so at TP = 2 the 7B bar on four GPUs halves against a fixed axis, from 126 GB to 63 GB. Its communication readout leaves out TP's all-reduces."]})]})]})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(o,{tab:"Pipeline Parallelism",plate:"ramelli-stacked-mills",children:[e.jsxs(a,{title:"Pipeline parallelism hands activations between stages",accent:6,number:"32",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
